<!-- ============ 1 ============ -->
<section id="answer">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2>The 30-second answer</h2></div>
  <p class="sec-sub">When they ask "what's your experience with OpenRouter?", this is the shape of it. Lead with the client-side vantage point — it's the one most candidates won't have.</p>

  <div class="card">
    <p class="say">"I've been on the other side of your API for about four months. I maintain Codewhale, an open-source terminal coding agent in Rust — around forty thousand stars, a hundred and eighty contributors. OpenRouter went in as a first-class provider in April after a community request, and in August I made it the documented default way to get started: install the agent, add one OpenRouter key, go. So I've had to model your catalog, your pricing feed, your provider-routing controls and your billing surface inside a harness that also talks to about forty other providers directly. Separately, I did a full supply-side diligence pass — pulling your <code>/endpoints</code> feed for a single model slug and pricing thirty hosts against DeepSeek direct, Fireworks, Vercel and Alibaba — to decide whether to build a metered inference product on top of you. Two things came out of that which I still think about: your unified reasoning-effort scale is the only provider surface I've integrated where I can pass a user's chosen effort straight through, and cost-optimal per-request routing is <em>not</em> cost-optimal per-trajectory, because the prefix cache doesn't move."</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Why that framing works</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>It puts you in the population OpenRouter cares most about and rarely gets to interview: <strong>a high-volume integrating app, not a chat user</strong>.</li>
    <li>It ends on two concrete, non-obvious technical claims, which invites the follow-up you most want ("say more about the prefix cache").</li>
    <li>It's true. Every clause has a receipt below.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ============ 2 ============ -->
<section id="receipts">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2>The receipts</h2></div>
  <p class="sec-sub">Dates and artifacts, so if anyone pushes on specifics you have them. Codewhale's first commit is 2026-01-20; OpenRouter arrived three months later and became the front door four months after that.</p>

  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What landed</th><th>Where</th><th>Tag</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="num">2026-04-27</td>
          <td>OpenRouter + Novita registered as first-class providers, in response to community issue&nbsp;#52. Shipped in v0.6.7.</td>
          <td class="k">f118db820</td>
          <td><span class="tag t-verified">verified</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="num">2026-06 →</td>
          <td>Live <code>/models</code> ingestion with full capability metadata — context, pricing, cache read <em>and</em> write, reasoning, tools, modalities — feeding the model picker and the offline seed.</td>
          <td class="k">#3385 · client.rs</td>
          <td><span class="tag t-verified">verified</span></td>
