<section id="stories">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>Five stories worth telling</h2></div>
  <p class="sec-sub">Each one is a setup, one specific detail that proves you were actually there, and the point it makes about you. Don't tell all five — pick by what the interviewer pulls on.</p>

  <div class="card story">
    <div class="story-head"><h3>1 · I made OpenRouter the front door</h3><span class="tag t-verified">verified</span></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Setup</span><p>Codewhale started as a DeepSeek-native agent. Onboarding meant picking a vendor before you'd run a single turn — the worst possible first decision to hand a new user.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">The move</span><p>On 2026-08-17 I rewrote the README lede, the guide, the website hero, the getting-started step, and the localized dictionaries around one line: <em>install Codewhale, add one OpenRouter key, and start working.</em> Direct provider keys and local models stayed first-class; OpenRouter became the shortest path in.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">The detail</span><p>I kept a copy test that asserts every command in the getting-started step also appears verbatim in the guide, so the two can't drift. Onboarding copy is a contract, not marketing.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Point</span><p>I've made the buy decision you want app developers to make, and I can articulate why: not price, but <em>one key across a catalog</em> removes the vendor commitment from the first five minutes.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="card story">
    <div class="story-head"><h3>2 · The reasoning-effort dialect matrix</h3><span class="tag t-verified">verified</span></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Setup</span><p>Codewhale exposes one user-facing control — <code>off / low / medium / high / xhigh</code> — and has to make it mean something on ~40 provider routes.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">The detail</span><p>Look at <code>apply_reasoning_effort</code> in <code>crates/tui/src/client.rs</code>. It's a giant match arm because every direct provider needs a different shape: <code>thinking:{type:"enabled"}</code>, <code>chat_template_kwargs.enable_thinking</code>, <code>think:true</code>, a nested <code>{type,effort}</code>, Anthropic's <code>budget_tokens</code>, or nothing at all because the gateway will 400 on an unknown field. Several DeepSeek-compatible hosts collapse low <em>and</em> medium to high because their wire contract was never published.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">The punchline</span><p>OpenRouter is the one arm where the user's actual choice passes through unmodified, because you normalized the scale to <code>none/minimal/low/medium/high/xhigh</code> across upstreams. That normalization is worth more to a harness author than the price aggregation, and I don't think it gets marketed as a feature.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Point</span><p>You know what the integration surface costs at the level of individual JSON fields, and you can name the specific thing OpenRouter does that saves you work.</p></div>
  </div>

