  <div class="card story">
    <div class="story-head"><h3>4 · Cost-optimal routing made the bill go up</h3><span class="tag t-analysis">analysis</span></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Setup</span><p>The prototype router was doing the obvious thing: sort candidate endpoints by cost, take the cheapest, bill cost-plus.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">The catch</span><p>Agent trajectories are prefix-cache businesses. In the Terminal-Bench run I instrumented, <strong>95.2M of 96.2M input tokens were cache reads</strong> — 98.9%. In the pilot's measured mix, cache-read share was 84.9% of input and output was 1.6% of input.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">The detail</span><p>Provider caches are not portable, and no gateway — yours or Vercel's — creates a shared cross-provider KV cache. So sorting by cost per request reshuffles the endpoint mid-trajectory, converts expected cache reads into fresh input at 5–10× the rate, and on a cost-plus bill the customer's total <em>rises</em> while the operator's take falls. I replaced it with trajectory stickiness and an explicit switch price.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Say it as</span><p>"Switch only when <code>savings &gt; P(cache&nbsp;miss) × reusable_prefix × (candidate_fresh − incumbent_cache_read) + latency penalty + failure×retry</code> — or when the incumbent goes unhealthy."</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Point</span><p>This is the single most research-shaped thing you have, it's specific to routers, and it's a live open problem at OpenRouter's scale.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="card story">
    <div class="story-head"><h3>5 · Fail closed on identity</h3><span class="tag t-verified">verified</span></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Setup</span><p>If you're going to charge someone a fixed rate for "DeepSeek V4 Flash 0731", you'd better be certain that's what served the request.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">The detail</span><p>The Worker sends <code>provider: { only, order, allow_fallbacks, require_parameters, quantizations:["fp8"] }</code>, then reads <code>openrouter_metadata.endpoints.available[].selected</code> and <code>usage.cost</code> back off the response to build a receipt naming who actually served it and what it cost. If the returned <code>model</code> field doesn't equal the pinned slug, the request fails rather than delivering — no silent cross-model fallback, ever.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Adjacent</span><p>I also learned the undated slug is a different model: <code>deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro</code> is the April 0423 build, not the 0813 one you meant. That's a foot-gun for anyone pinning revisions.</p></div>
    <div class="beat"><span class="beat-label">Point</span><p>You use the routing controls the way a serious integrator does, and you built observability on top of the metadata OpenRouter already returns.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

