<section id="api">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2>What the API taught me</h2></div>
  <p class="sec-sub">Fourteen concrete things, each of which cost real debugging time. This is the table to skim on the way in — any one of them proves the integration was real.</p>

  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>What I learned</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="k">/api/v1/auth/key</td><td><strong>You can't validate a key with <code>/models</code>.</strong> It returns 200 for any bearer, including a bogus one. <code>/auth/key</code> is the canonical who-am-I: 200 for valid, 401 otherwise. Both the TypeScript and Rust integrations use it for the login flow.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">pricing.*</td><td>Prices arrive as <strong>per-token USD strings</strong>. Multiply by 1e6 for per-million, and parse them as strings first — they're not numbers in the JSON.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">input_cache_write</td><td>Easy to miss, and dropping it <strong>undercounts every cache-creation turn</strong> on the upstreams that charge a write premium (Anthropic, Qwen). It's a separate field from <code>input_cache_read</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">top_provider</td><td><code>top_provider.context_length</code> and <code>max_completion_tokens</code> are the numbers that matter. The model-level <code>context_length</code> is a ceiling across all endpoints, not what a given route will give you.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">supported_parameters</td><td>The honest capability oracle. Codewhale derives "supports reasoning" and "supports tools" from this array rather than from name-matching, which is the thing that breaks on every new model launch.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">architecture.modality</td><td>When <code>input_modalities</code>/<code>output_modalities</code> are empty, the <code>"text-&gt;text"</code> string is the fallback. Two shapes for one fact.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">reasoning_effort</td><td>The unified <code>none/minimal/low/medium/high/xhigh</code> scale is <strong>the only provider surface where a user's chosen effort passes straight through</strong> in a 40-provider harness. See story 2.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">:free suffix</td><td>A suffix is not a different model family. <code>deepseek-chat:free</code> still hits V4 server-side and still needs <code>reasoning_content</code> replay. A prefix/alias matcher that stops at the colon will silently corrupt thinking traces.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">provider{}</td><td><code>only</code>, <code>order</code>, <code>allow_fallbacks</code>, <code>require_parameters</code>, <code>quantizations</code> — these controls are the reason a product with a price promise can use a router at all. <code>require_parameters</code> especially: it drops endpoints that would silently ignore a field you sent.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">openrouter_metadata</td><td><code>endpoints.available[].selected.provider</code> plus <code>usage.cost</code> gives you a per-request receipt of who served it and what it cost. Good enough to reconcile a ledger against.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">HTTP 402</td><td>Credits exhausted — distinct from 429 (upstream rate limit). Different customer-facing message, different retry policy. Worth mapping separately.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">HTTP-Referer / X-Title</td><td>The only path onto the app rankings; there's no manual submission. I scoped them to OpenRouter routes only and let a user-configured header of the same name override — they identify the app, never the user.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">billing surface</td><td>Aggregator prices are the <em>aggregator's</em> catalog, not the model owner's rate card. Codewhale models <code>aggregator-payg</code> as a distinct billing surface from <code>first-party-payg</code> so cost display fails closed instead of quoting DeepSeek's numbers for an OpenRouter route.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="k">capability inheritance</td><td>A capability doesn't transfer through a gateway by name similarity. <code>openrouter/openai/gpt-5.6</code> is pinned as <code>Unknown</code> for server-side web search, not <code>Supported</code>, because the first-party feature isn't necessarily on the routed path. Same for cache telemetry — the OpenRouter DeepSeek route is <code>thinking_supported = true, cache_telemetry_supported = false</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 5 ============ -->
<section id="economics">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">05</span><h2>What the economics taught me</h2></div>
  <p class="sec-sub">This is the material that separates you from someone who just called the API. It came out of deciding whether to resell inference, which is a question OpenRouter has already answered for itself.</p>

  <h3>The fee is real and it is not the point</h3>
  <p>The 5.5% credit-purchase fee ($0.80 minimum) moved a DeepInfra-sourced gross margin from 42.6% to 39.4%, and raised COGS on the modeled Flash card from 57.5% to 60.7% of retail. <span class="tag t-measured">measured</span></p>
  <p>What I concluded, and would say in the room: <strong>for a US developer tool, the access argument beat the price argument.</strong> OpenRouter reached StreamLake and Baidu Qianfan — two of the best margins on the board — from one US account with one card and no Chinese business verification. StreamLake's console is mainland-only; Baidu's international arm wants business verification. Three percentage points of fee to avoid two foreign entity signups is not a close call. <span class="tag t-analysis">analysis</span></p>

  <h3>Catalog availability is not a supply contract</h3>
  <p>Between the 2026-08-19 memo and a 2026-08-20 refresh of the same endpoint feed, rows had already drifted. The rule I wrote for myself: a catalog row is a claim on a date; each host stays a separate <em>supply product</em> until a qualification suite proves revision hash, tokenizer, chat template, sampling defaults, tool-call schema, context and max-output behavior, cache counters/TTL/isolation, precision, region, retention, capacity, and settlement rate. <span class="tag t-verified">verified</span></p>

  <h3>Agentic traffic is a cache-read business</h3>
  <div class="receipt">
<b>Measured, Terminal-Bench 2.1 leg (Codewhale 0.9.7, 2026-08-23)</b>
input tokens ......... 96,204,712
of which cache reads .. 95,205,888   (98.96%)
output tokens ......... 1,676,829   (1.74% of input)

<b>Modeled pilot mix</b>
cache-read share of input .. 84.95%
output / input ratio ....... 1.59%
  </div>
  <p>Two implications I'd offer. First, per-token headline price is a weak predictor of what an agent workload actually costs — the cache rate dominates. Second, an endpoint that <em>reports</em> cache tokens is worth more than a cheaper one that doesn't, because you can't manage what you can't see. I built a cache canary for exactly this: send an identical 200K-token prefix twice inside 60 seconds, require a non-zero cache-hit counter <em>and</em> a matching invoice line, and re-run it daily as a circuit breaker rather than a one-time gate. <span class="tag t-analysis">analysis</span></p>

  <h3>One unresolved thread, worth mentioning as one</h3>
  <p>OpenRouter described Flash 0731 as 13B active / 284B total; the Hugging Face model card cited a 304B checkpoint. I flagged it as a reconciliation item and never closed it. Bringing an <em>open</em> question is stronger than pretending you closed it. <span class="tag t-open">open</span></p>
</section>

<!-- ============ 6 ============ -->
<section id="research">
  <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">06</span><h2>The research angle</h2></div>
  <p class="sec-sub">The role is Research Scientist, so the integration stories are the credential and this is the pitch. You already wrote a routing-evaluation contract at Arcee — reuse its spine.</p>

  <h3>The methodology you already have</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Three separate lanes, so routing gains can't be confounded.</strong> A controlled-harness lane (one pinned model, provider, settings, tool policy and budget for every harness), a plan-realistic lane, and a routing-ablation lane. Routing is measured against a fixed fallback, a validation-selected fixed model, the auto router, and a post-hoc oracle. <span class="tag t-verified">verified</span></li>
    <li><strong>The oracle is a ceiling, never a score.</strong> It picks the best route after seeing every task's outcome, so it bounds the headroom and is not reportable.</li>
    <li><strong>Provider identity is first-class.</strong> Every result records provider, effective model, and revision — because hosts serve and route nominally identical IDs differently.</li>
    <li><strong>The exact line I wrote about OpenRouter's role:</strong> it "can be a transport in the controlled lane because it makes same-model experiments convenient. It is not the definition of the product plan and does not make models comparable by itself." That's a fair, non-flattering framing that will land better than praise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The harness-effect result</h3>
  <p>On 2026-08-23 I ran Terminal-Bench 2.1 — 89 tasks, one attempt each, fresh container per trial, same neutral prompt wrapper, no subagents or project instructions — with the <em>model held completely fixed</em> across two different agent harnesses, to isolate harness effect from model effect. <span class="tag t-measured">measured</span></p>
  <div class="receipt">
<b>Snapshot 2026-08-24T00:44Z — IN PROGRESS, not a final result</b>
Codewhale 0.9.7 ... 44 scored · 35 pass · 9 fail · 5 timeout · mean reward 0.795 · 43 pending
Pi 0.84.2 ......... 28 scored · 19 pass · 9 fail · 7 timeout · mean reward 0.679 · 59 pending
  </div>
  <p>Present this as <em>evidence of method</em>, not as a headline. The legs are at different completion depths and roughly half the suite is unfinished; quoting the means as a verdict would be exactly the sin the methodology exists to prevent. Saying that out loud is the point.</p>

  <h3>Four things I'd want to work on there</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Endpoint identity as a measurement problem.</strong> Turn "same slug" from an assumption into a continuously-tested property: revision attestation, tokenizer and chat-template build, sampling defaults, tool-call schema conformance, context and max-output behavior under load, cache counter semantics and TTL, serving precision. OpenRouter is the only party with the traffic to run this as a standing suite rather than a one-off audit.</li>
    <li><strong>Cache-aware routing.</strong> Formalize the trajectory switch cost, measure realized cache-hit rate per endpoint rather than assuming the advertised discount, and make stickiness a first-class routing objective alongside price and latency. This is story 4, scaled.</li>
    <li><strong>Quantization as an unpriced axis.</strong> fp4 and fp8 endpoints sit in the same list at a 2× price spread and no published quality delta on agentic tasks. Nobody outside OpenRouter can answer what that spread actually buys.</li>
    <li><strong>Harness effect as a confound in app rankings.</strong> The rankings measure app × model. A controlled-harness lane would let OpenRouter say something about model quality that no leaderboard currently can — and I've built the instrumentation for exactly that.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

